edb/ref 17592186310041} {:answer/id #uuid "5f22f9b6-81bd-56d0-9b67-07da7fade9d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310042} {:answer/id #uuid "cad0487a-74d1-5cd4-aa47-9af37019ec15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310043} {:answer/id #uuid "31e7b61e-ad24-56b3-8592-beab7e97ae53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "467e7d7c-86ae-56e9-bfad-85b91e347ceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310041} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0a9012a-a9d2-50e6-ab09-0507e061c49b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf049654-9607-5548-9f2e-3fc78c899d58" :question/math-academy-id "q-106402" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/ea3bc202dd0a40cfe096fb4170331fed39de28870aa355c776fd82de3b5db26b.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/74714486ad225aba7111329be0c3853b7b922a852df450b057632e73fce5be6d.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "698cb1f5-9f40-502e-9c6c-d125f336a37d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309943} {:answer/id #uuid "334bb100-678c-551c-b431-5ad508a48042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309944} {:answer/id #uuid "d1619ef5-bb2e-58c9-8361-e4c22b35dd95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309945} {:answer/id #uuid "1f096eca-93a6-5e7a-a73c-8a6d3e69b7a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309946} {:answer/id #uuid "994a8f87-54d3-56eb-a26c-246d8d55a67b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "334bb100-678c-551c-b431-5ad508a48042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309944} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6788439-9f18-5be1-81dc-e9244ab289ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4db2471c-43eb-5703-bc68-40fdaaff995e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106406" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 3y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 9{y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad88058-c875-5036-b4ea-3628069695fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287333} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb03b3c-e66b-5556-847f-39b8e859dc0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287334} {:answer/id #uuid "432d428a-efcb-5ece-9091-69f2afa8dec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287335} {:answer/id #uuid "3c92de15-0997-5844-af75-b5727a650d7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287336} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f59f1d-6c3d-54ad-867d-b18963ce5dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ad88058-c875-5036-b4ea-3628069695fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287333} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad1b808d-be7f-533e-807d-3c989cce7046" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86ea583d-a580-5671-b8bf-22431844e7be" :question/math-academy-id "q-15843" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bf77ad8-e90f-5318-8684-5d94c505e0ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bf77ad8-e90f-5318-8684-5d94c505e0ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287340} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e675469-7ed3-50ca-b8c4-63856f23fd96" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287339} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "775dbfe2-7347-5fb3-9641-24c4c41065e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sin(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "775dbfe2-7347-5fb3-9641-24c4c41065e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sin(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287342} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c750959-1bdb-5f24-93a8-046ad41f914d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8dcce0e-e879-55c5-84f2-41534e0d01fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-274855" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/d906e74fdd78e074ab410d1042c134c892fd1b50b78b0f50a76ccf2ee203c203.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(6,0).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec70913a-e3c1-5064-b2ae-9b2f7ac13877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cos(\\theta)-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310047} {:answer/id #uuid "87a81455-0e3f-5c10-b5f1-cf65ea179559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cos(\\theta)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87a81455-0e3f-5c10-b5f1-cf65ea179559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cos(\\theta)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310048} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ff0224-5789-5183-bc35-dd90401402e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310046} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9798a0d-0e38-5fdf-a146-00a285c0efe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin(\\theta)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9798a0d-0e38-5fdf-a146-00a285c0efe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin(\\theta)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310050} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d7dc6fc-1da8-5c38-9a57-98e8de73651d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6f3de744-1bd8-5199-b4f6-0d2541d24c48" :question/math-academy-id "q-274862" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/c9340314acbaab9f4b4e9180e3028848ad80e8fa33e26bff32eaf47b00d164a2.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(2,-1)$ with horizontal radius $5$ and vertical radius $3,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-1).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(2,-1),$ $a=5$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c6293e4-9854-5573-bf7d-9e24a1783466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310053} {:answer/id #uuid "e49cf9d3-8f8f-5391-9c46-134a4623bc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e49cf9d3-8f8f-5391-9c46-134a4623bc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310054} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c06a867-8aec-5c8d-9356-f28823c4cb07" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310052} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b4ef3b-37d6-5da9-9e69-998f6842c384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01b4ef3b-37d6-5da9-9e69-998f6842c384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310056} :answer-field/id #uuid "b841ca81-4a11-52d1-9932-a7be61e71c45" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0040dab2-1f55-5b75-9bcf-2c5e4bdaee49" :question/math-academy-id "q-274866" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/061c7c34639d88b72d1a3045e794e2aa149b3331dafa33447071aed80b861088.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the ellipse.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/6dc795d39f90b6df177fe815a9135e7eadbe0b0d7d01214009de495e704486f7.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3233f5de-29c3-5b81-a289-815356f700f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3233f5de-29c3-5b81-a289-815356f700f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310059} :answer-field/id #uuid "6836ee91-2196-5abe-8d87-442849908878" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310058} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65685c40-555c-5428-b23d-3a3844a0da86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sin(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65685c40-555c-5428-b23d-3a3844a0da86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sin(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310061} :answer-field/id #uuid "9160bfd8-08a2-59b0-ac63-202c2c2e9e56" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e757c132-c765-59f3-be49-296fcd0ef862" :question/math-academy-id "q-326267" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/3497daefa76dd730942b7deb1f32c42a30823c9de953f45ec011218aff126b21.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $9,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $J(2,0).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=9.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24554dd2-c7a7-5795-ace8-ef8ee8123e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287345} {:answer/id #uuid "5a761898-639e-58fc-b2fd-c7718ff280db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287346} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6b88a5-43fb-5246-b8fd-808ca702cf9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24554dd2-c7a7-5795-ace8-ef8ee8123e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287345} :answer-field/id #uuid "2071a2f6-650b-5c57-86b4-17df74f93151" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287344} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66173e3f-74f3-53c0-ad15-c0b28d4d1d6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sin(\\theta)+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66173e3f-74f3-53c0-ad15-c0b28d4d1d6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sin(\\theta)+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287348} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f6c69d3-7405-5114-a936-18767e95e99d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ca5b2e3e-ddb1-5792-98f6-dd4e9edd115c" :question/math-academy-id "q-327171" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/61ffc8879681a1d0bb381f32178ea2e7b58e251341b2280ba607448231458dc5.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(0,5)$ with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $8,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,5).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(0,5),$ $a=3$ and $b=8.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37a96b6f-90f6-5067-a4e1-61d8a9d34747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^2}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^2}{99}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37a96b6f-90f6-5067-a4e1-61d8a9d34747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^2}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^2}{99}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310065} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe81c360-ef75-5866-b642-fff70e9b7d28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c978aef-b5f2-571e-91d6-0c640846f534" :question/math-academy-id "q-327803" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\sqrt{11}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +12,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter  $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4 \\\\ \\frac{x+4}{10} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{{10}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{11}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +12 \\\\ \\frac{y-12}{3\\sqrt{11}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{11})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13dc05ed-4d2a-54f7-9143-1df7dfaf56b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{96}+\\frac{y^2}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13dc05ed-4d2a-54f7-9143-1df7dfaf56b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{96}+\\frac{y^2}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309969} :answer-field/id #uuid "a68d4dad-6dbc-5905-9104-5cbbef20ce2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a17ad85-75b3-57c6-af87-8cba05167eb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-327808" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4\\sqrt{6}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(4\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{96} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{5\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{50} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19ad4ec0-c881-5ae7-a382-6cc3818c0abe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19ad4ec0-c881-5ae7-a382-6cc3818c0abe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310068} :answer-field/id #uuid "7227bee1-b461-5fd2-9c93-bbe1d1ef17de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310067} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7200d9ef-eca3-5310-a6aa-8dcdc8e46cf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7200d9ef-eca3-5310-a6aa-8dcdc8e46cf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310070} :answer-field/id #uuid "50cd9554-87d7-53e9-8226-f536e8c03638" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70bf7d5a-49a5-578b-bafa-224f26540a89" :question/math-academy-id "q-327822" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/31b10310b585d867ecf883db2a5d778ec5ec9160df03fc4487d2ef7fd0b9a3af.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/73cbae2c39fb2466fd59ced196c08ff2b6131b08a6a5dd4933e736404b12266e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fdaf5a5-fce3-56ec-af9d-3d07ff93b92f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fdaf5a5-fce3-56ec-af9d-3d07ff93b92f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310073} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bd7e3df-4fde-5785-b280-a77314580cef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310072} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a93c1fdc-81e0-548e-8bb7-d374cb5c868e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a93c1fdc-81e0-548e-8bb7-d374cb5c868e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310075} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b647bdb-2df2-500e-8d81-f14155ea2163" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b60b26b2-8291-58d7-a30a-dee3279193ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-328327" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/5234672d8176a0a04bbc3feecfe13092ff116a25682ae055286f1fe858b8a754.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-1,2),$ $a=5,$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the horizontal line through its center.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/1b2d7617af37197dfd2702b4497263e563458b0bf11c622308220def93b688a9.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}],$ we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "582cfdb3-9e7d-5f15-a26a-d00ec9f67751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287370} {:answer/id #uuid "cd12cacf-3474-5a8a-8a58-48b6696f9204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287371} {:answer/id #uuid "69ea148e-dfc2-5dd4-bbfe-d1130acc22ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287372} {:answer/id #uuid "3a98dff9-85fe-5c45-bae9-9e80401d71b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287373} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a87621-cfad-513c-8bc6-4e1fddf2fcfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9a87621-cfad-513c-8bc6-4e1fddf2fcfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287374} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec9984a4-cf5d-59d7-aabc-48c476f6a41a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99d45658-64c1-527b-ba91-3ee0b91560a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-6880" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/86dcfa13b7863c08b348855e776a6df709ecac499af02c2479cfdf484eeaf328.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b591b959-0fa6-5a9f-9c27-2d53c19f3ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + y^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150577} {:answer/id #uuid "d6492c8b-6cd1-5aa0-99f1-a1b0cdabe68a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + y^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150578} {:answer/id #uuid "feb74112-2dcd-5dc5-9b05-85e93ac4fe66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + y^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150579} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4b28d0-0159-54f3-bdb2-0d6d0d566c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150580} {:answer/id #uuid "69563d64-d0ff-507a-b25a-bf11edff79ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{4} - y^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b591b959-0fa6-5a9f-9c27-2d53c19f3ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + y^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150577} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b6f6f0a-f8bc-5eb3-93b0-03987e8b9e8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be3693e3-b291-55cc-9e6f-d294ae6aac43" :question/math-academy-id "q-6881" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97aba8b1-4d57-59f1-8681-27a6f5ef87df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 4\\cos \\theta  + 3, y = 6\\sin \\theta  - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151213} {:answer/id #uuid "c45d0cc5-7ee8-542f-807b-60210f8560ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 4\\sin \\theta  + 3, y = 6\\cos \\theta  - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151214} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ed8c91-be29-513e-b16d-df6bd83fd9d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\cos \\theta  - 3, y = \\sin \\theta  + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151215} {:answer/id #uuid "094d6df1-4230-52c5-a868-37f8f1c16aab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2\\sin \\theta  + 3, y = 3\\cos \\theta  - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151216} {:answer/id #uuid "576033fc-0922-5555-a8fc-419643c57fd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2\\cos \\theta  + 3, y = 3\\sin \\theta  - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "576033fc-0922-5555-a8fc-419643c57fd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2\\cos \\theta  + 3, y = 3\\sin \\theta  - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151217} :answer-field/id #uuid "85479bab-2698-5739-baf8-d6800ff00faa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c09e84a-e7d0-5c50-95f2-01ada1064320" :question/math-academy-id "q-78680" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/14d1fc5a92c28cc7f84c1ec23929701f1aca67b1a15d6113fa83f1428354fc11.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,-1)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $3,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,-1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95eae759-7706-57f4-88d2-511815ad1f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287384} {:answer/id #uuid "3826e7f2-0ee1-5ea7-be8b-75e912241519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287385} {:answer/id #uuid "f263f1c5-c023-52c5-b17c-3b36f00f05e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287386} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4f07ea-3198-56ba-ae69-7e96fd25c5f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287387} {:answer/id #uuid "5ab376a3-3e60-5149-ae06-fc3d45c3c97f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c4f07ea-3198-56ba-ae69-7e96fd25c5f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287387} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e87eca0-e8ca-545a-beab-0c3a297aa01b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9798ed5-9467-5b84-a966-140686dec581" :question/math-academy-id "q-79281" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/ac2bba143473bc68e3a578c008a5de3cc36727d984bfba5d5e0da95462c08f77.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f6c5b8-b3c4-5f6c-84ab-f21cd125a6e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310078} {:answer/id #uuid "c111a511-c413-5c58-8f29-e47f92a50995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310079} {:answer/id #uuid "013dd0b0-04cc-5fc1-8c28-33a4652bcac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310080} {:answer/id #uuid "e862eba8-1591-5ddb-b9ec-4a577375635a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310081} {:answer/id #uuid "cf59d33a-02ad-573c-bc13-343534c52cbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "013dd0b0-04cc-5fc1-8c28-33a4652bcac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310080} :answer-field/id #uuid "71b4f063-809e-5257-aa5b-18d2d3d6c6c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8be779c-3919-56bb-aa84-c33b693d19f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79290" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/e5d32d7b081238d3717a03f7718fcf8e3de8a7a00e5a04eada0129d1bcc43dc3.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-2,5)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,5)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,5),$ $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}]}
